## 2026-10-05 23:35 third-party 32-step witness (yudduy, PR #227 = submission 8f95560) verified locally
- We fetched the certificate bytes from PR #227 (head 689cb95e) into the scratchpad only. They are not copied into
  our package and are not used as our certificate.
- Organizer `digest(., 'sha256', r)` on the two 128-byte messages:
  - r = 32: both f8a3db111360e5ed2e63041ecfa82b01c95a25882910bc0f21671949296a4e77, a COLLISION;
  - r = 31, 35, 64: the digests differ.
  - M0 is shared; M1 differs in W4..W8, W12, W13 with exactly the Fig. 6 XOR masks.
- With message-b as the unprimed side and CV1 = C_32(IV, M0) = e890c4ba 6bce94e6 47a0b812 c5ef36b2 ec7b7814 91cf09df
  a9717904 494bc86f: every signed row A, E (-4..22) and W (0..31) equals Fig. 6 (0 mismatches). It is a real 32-step
  instance of exactly this truncated characteristic, with a C_32 first block.
  (`r32_search/yudduy_check/{vw.py,conf.py}`)
- Their terminal counts give independent rates:
  - 14,643,237 admitted tuples from 2,405,181,685,760 first blocks = 6.088e-6 = 2^-17.326, against our q = 2^-17.337;
  - 1 collision in 2,799,733,071,213 = 2^41.35 tuple-tail pairs. Under p = 2^-46 the chance of a success by then
    is about 0.04; under 2^-44 about 0.16.
  - So their single success is consistent with p >= 2^-46. It is one event, not a rate.
- Their 2^61.9 is a prospective cap (8,192 matcher attempts x 2^36 first-block slots, plus 2^31 tuple records x all
  tails, plus a rerun allowance, with per-slot op caps of 2^24 and 2^16). It is not an expected or observed cost.
  Their observed run (2^41.1 first blocks, 2^41.35 tail pairs) sits below our T = 2^45 trials. Our T comes from
  P >= 0.43 at q_LCB and p = 2^-46.
- Their score of 64.3 is dominated by a 2^64 allowance: the paper's 2^48.335 attack total, rounded to 2^49 and
  inflated by C. It contains no measurement of the route search, which is what this package supplies.

